WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.960
Today we are going to learn why you should&nbsp;
not even try to handcuff a computer graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:18.800
researcher. And it’s not because they are criminal&nbsp;
masterminds, no-no! So what is it then? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.600
if they have read this paper, handcuffing them&nbsp;
will not work. See? They will get out easily.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:32.880
So, what is all this insanity here? Well,&nbsp;
this paper is about simulating repulsion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:40.400
Or, to be more exact, computing repulsive&nbsp;
forces on curves and surfaces. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.680
of course, your first question is,&nbsp;
okay, but what is that good for?

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:52.160
Well, believe it or not, it doesn’t sound&nbsp;
like it, but it is good for so many things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.880
the applications just keep coming and coming.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.560
So, how does this work? Well, repulsion&nbsp;
can prevent intersections and collisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:11.520
And, look at that! If we run this process over&nbsp;
time, it creates flows. In other words, it starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:18.080
mingling with the geometry in interesting,&nbsp;
and it turns out, also, useful ways.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.960
For instance, imagine that we have this&nbsp;
tangled handcuff. And someone comes up to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:32.480
and says they can untangle this object by&nbsp;
molding, and not only that, but this person gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.280
even more brazen: they say they even do&nbsp;
it gently, or in other words, without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:44.800
any intersections. No colliding&nbsp;
or breaking is allowed. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.040
then I say I don’t believe a word of it,&nbsp;
show me! And the person does exactly that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:58.400
Using the repulsive force algorithm, we can find a&nbsp;
way to untangle them. This seems like black magic.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:06.480
With some adjustments to this process, and running&nbsp;
it backwards in time, we can even start with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:13.680
piece of geometry, and compute an ideal shrink&nbsp;
wrap for it. Even better, when applied to point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:21.040
clouds, we can even create a full 3D geometry&nbsp;
that represents these points. And, get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:30.560
even if the point clouds are incomplete! Make no&nbsp;
mistake, the topic of point cloud reconstruction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:37.920
has been studied for many decades now, so much so&nbsp;
that during my PhD years, I attended well over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:45.760
hundred paper talks on this topic. And I am by&nbsp;
no means an expert on this, not even close, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:53.040
I can tell you, this looks like a pretty good&nbsp;
solution. And these applications emerge only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:03:00.960
a tiny side effect of this algorithm. But&nbsp;
it’s not over. There is more! It can even fix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:07.920
bad mesh geometries, something that artists&nbsp;
encounter in the wild all the time. Loving it!

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.160
And, it can also create cool and crazy&nbsp;
surfaces for your art installations.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.840
Now, so far, we have applied repulsion to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:25.520
surfaces. But this concept can also be applied&nbsp;
to curves as well, which opens up a whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.560
new world of really cool applications.&nbsp;
Let’s start with the most important one.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.720
Do you know what happens when you put&nbsp;
your wired earbuds into your pocket?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:43.680
Yes! Of course, exactly this happens. Every&nbsp;
time, right? And now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:49.120
because it turns out, it can even untangle&nbsp;
your headphones without breaking them.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:57.360
Now wait a second…if it can generate curves&nbsp;
that don’t intersect, maybe it could also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:04.400
used for path planning for characters in a&nbsp;
virtual world. Look, these folks intersect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.760
But if they use this new technique for path&nbsp;
planning - there should be no intersections. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.200
yes! No intersections, thereby,&nbsp;
no collisions. Excellent.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.880
And if we feel like it, we can also start with&nbsp;
a small piece of noodle inside a bunny and start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:32.960
growing it. Why not? And then, we can marvel at&nbsp;
how over time, it starts to look like intestines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.960
And it just still keeps growing and growing&nbsp;
without touching the bunny. So cool!

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:47.520
Now, onto more useful things. It can even be&nbsp;
used to visualize social media connections and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:56.160
family trees in a compact manner. Or, it can move&nbsp;
muscle fibers around. And the list just goes on.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:03.040
But you are experienced Fellow Scholars over&nbsp;
there, so I hear you asking, well, wait a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:10.000
all this sounds quite trivial. Just apply&nbsp;
repulsive forces to the entirety of the surfaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:16.240
and off we go. Why does this have to be a paper&nbsp;
published at a prestigious conference? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:24.880
actually, let’s try this. If we do that…&nbsp;
uh-oh. This happens. Or this happens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.880
This algorithm is not so trivial after all!

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:35.840
And this is what I love about this paper. It&nbsp;
proposes a simple idea. And this idea is simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:44.160
but not easy. If we think it is easy, this&nbsp;
happens. If we do it well, this happens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.400
The paper describes in detail how to perform&nbsp;
this so it works properly, and provides a ton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:58.000
of immediate things it can be used to. This is not&nbsp;
a product, this is an idea and a list of potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:06.560
applications for it. In my opinion, this is basic&nbsp;
academic research at its best. Bravo! I love it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.040
And, what would you use this for? Please&nbsp;
let me know in the comments below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:18.000
I’d love to hear your ideas. And until then, we&nbsp;
will have no more tangled earbuds in our pockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.720
and we can thank computer graphics&nbsp;
research for that. What a time to be alive!

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.160
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

